---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { stats } from "../lib/entries";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site";
import { href, count } from "../lib/utils";

const s = await stats();
const hasContent = s.problems + s.theory > 0;
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description={`About ${SITE.name} â€” what it is, how it is built, and how each entry is written up.`}
  topics={s.topics}
  subtopics={s.subtopics}
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="page-head__eyebrow">Colophon</p>
    <h1 class="page-head__title">About this vault</h1>
    <p class="page-head__lede">
      A personal mathematics vault: problems and theory I want to keep, written up
      properly enough that future-me can relearn from them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What is here</h2>
    <p>
      Competition material â€” JEE Advanced, RMO, INMO, IMO, Putnam, integration bee
      â€” alongside undergraduate-level theory. Every problem entry records the
      source, the <strong>key idea</strong> that unlocks it, and a complete
      solution. Every theory entry records a statement, a proof, when to reach for
      the tool, and where it fails.
    </p>
    <p>
      Currently {count(s.problems, "problem", "problems")} and{" "}
      {count(s.theory, "theory note", "theory notes")} across {count(s.topics.length, "topic", "topics")}.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is written</h2>
    <p>
      Two rules, both learned the hard way:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>State the key idea before the solution.</strong> A solution you can
        read is not the same as a problem you can solve. The one line that makes the
        problem fall over is the part worth remembering.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Write down what is wrong or unfinished.</strong> If a proof step is
        hand-waved, if a transcription looks inconsistent with the official source,
        if a construction was abandoned halfway â€” that goes in the entry, not in a
        silent rewrite. The gaps are information.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      Completely static. Every entry is a Markdown file with YAML frontmatter, kept
      in a git repository. A build step turns those files into HTML with mathematics
      pre-rendered to KaTeX at build time, so there is no MathJax script and no
      layout shift. There is no database and no server-side code.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Content: <code>content/problems/**.md</code> and <code>content/theory/**.md</code></li>
      <li>Engine: Astro, with <code>remark-math</code> and <code>rehype-katex</code></li>
      <li>Search: a static Pagefind index built from the HTML</li>
      <li>Editing: a local admin panel, reachable only from my machine</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Because the site is static, there is nothing to log into and nothing to
      attack: the admin panel is a separate process bound to localhost and never
      deployed. Readers get HTML.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections and sources</h2>
    <p>
      Problems remain the property of their original sources â€” contests, textbooks
      and their authors. What is mine here is the exposition: the choice of key
      idea, the solutions, and the commentary. Where a problem is transcribed from
      a competition, the source is named, and any doubt about the transcription is
      flagged in the entry itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Found an error? The repository is at{" "}
      <a href={SITE.repo} rel="noopener noreferrer">{SITE.repo.replace(/^https?:\/\//, "")}</a>
      â€” an issue or a pull request is welcome.
    </p>

    {!hasContent && (
      <p class="empty">
        This vault is still empty. Add Markdown files under <code>content/</code>,
        or run <code>pnpm admin</code> to start writing.
      </p>
    )}
  </div>
</Base>
